  "response": "When a structure fails and the parties responsible for it point at one another, forensic engineering turns an adversarial dispute into a question that physical evidence can answer. The Riverside pedestrian bridge shows how this works. The central span of the Riverside pedestrian bridge collapsed in March 2019. Although Halvorsen Structural argued that the collapse was caused by deficient construction, Corbett Construction argued that the collapse was caused by a design error. Neither position can be judged from the statements of the parties. Each firm has an obvious interest in the outcome, and each can cite documents that favor its own reading. The investigator's task is therefore to set aside the competing narratives and rebuild the failure sequence from the steel, the drawings, and the record of who changed what.\n\nThe first step is to locate where failure began, since a collapse spreads outward from an initiating point and the conditions at that point carry the most diagnostic weight. The structural failure initiated at a hanger rod connection on the box beam. Forensic engineers recovered the failed connection hardware from the debris. Provided that this hardware was in hand, they could compare the as-built connections against the approved design drawings, and they did so. Fracture surfaces, deformation patterns, bolt and weld geometry, and plate dimensions can only be measured on recovered material. Without it, any comparison with the design documents would depend on photographs and testimony. That comparison indicates that the hanger rod connection was fabricated differently from the original design drawings. The departure has a documented origin: the as-built detail diverged because a shop drawing submitted by Corbett Construction altered the hanger rod connection detail. Shop drawings are the contractor's translation of the design into fabrication instructions. When one modifies a connection, the investigator must establish whether the designer reviewed and approved the change, because that review is what spreads responsibility across both parties.\n\nA finding of deviation does not end the inquiry, because the investigator must also test whether the original design would have performed adequately had it been built as drawn. This is done by an independent structural analysis of the connection under the governing load combinations. The hanger rod connection was undersized in the original design. At least one of these defects, perhaps both, therefore contributed to the failure: either the undersizing in the original detail or the fabrication departure from it. This is precisely why such disputes rarely end in a clean verdict for either side. The material record matters as well. The hanger rods on the bridge were fabricated from aluminum, and that choice of material caused the failure to initiate at that connection. Metallurgical testing of the recovered rods, including coupon tests and chemical analysis, lets the investigator check the specified material against what was actually installed.\n\nThe circumstances of loading at the time of failure also bear on the analysis, since they determine which load cases the investigator must reconstruct. On this point the record admits only one of two accounts, and one of them must be wrong: either the bridge had been open to the public before the collapse, or the bridge had never been opened to the public before the collapse. The collapse occurred during a load test conducted before the bridge opened, which confirms the second account, that the span had never carried public traffic. The bridge was closed to all traffic after that load-test collapse. In other words, after the collapse, authorities barred every user from the bridge. This preserved the site and the remaining structure for continued examination.\n\nThe method this case illustrates applies generally. Investigators find the initiation point, recover and test the failed components, compare as-built conditions against approved documents, trace every alteration to its author and its approval, and independently check the adequacy of the original design. Responsibility follows from that chain of physical and documentary evidence rather than from the accusations of the parties. Where the chain shows defects in both design and construction, the engineering finding will allocate fault to both, whatever either firm has claimed.",
